Proposition 1.1. In any group G:
the identity is unique;
each element has exactly one inverse, written a−1;
(a−1)−1=a and (ab)−1=b−1a−1;
cancellation: ab=ac implies b=c, and ba=ca implies b=c;
the equations ax=b and ya=b have the unique solutions x=a−1b and y=ba−1.
Proof. (1) If e and e′ are identities, e=ee′=e′. (2) If b and c are inverses of a, then b=be=b(ac)=(ba)c=ec=c. (3) a satisfies the definition of an inverse of a−1, so by (2) it is the inverse. For the product, (ab)(b−1a−1)=a(bb−1)a−1=aa−1=e, and similarly in the other order. (4) Multiply ab=ac on the left by a−1 and use associativity. (5) x=a−1b works; if x′ also works, ax=ax′ and cancellation gives x=x′. ■
Common pitfall: (ab)−1=b−1a−1, not a−1b−1 (the "socks and shoes" rule: to undo putting on socks then shoes, remove the shoes first). The two agree only when a and b commute.
Powers are defined as usual: a0=e, an=a⋯a (n factors), a−n=(a−1)n. The laws aman=am+n and (am)n=amn hold in every group, but (ab)n=anbn can fail when ab≠ba. In additive notation an becomes na.
